course: dictionary
lesson: 13639
title: Words too great to count
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe things so valuable or great that nobody could put a number on them. Inestimable is one of these words. It is formal, so you will often meet it in speeches, letters and history books.
explain: Break the word into parts to work out its meaning: in- means not, estimate means a judgement of size or value, and -able means can be. So something inestimable is too great to measure. Test it in the sentence: «Your help has been of inestimable value.» Could anyone count that help exactly? No, so it must be extremely valuable.
explain: Look for clue words around it, such as value, importance or worth. They show that inestimable is praising something. Then try your meaning in the sentence: «The museum holds treasures of inestimable worth.» If priceless fits and the sentence still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: Inestimable means too great to be measured or counted.
fact: Inestimable also means extremely valuable or important, as in «of inestimable value».
fact: Inestimable is a formal word, so it is often used in speeches and thank-you notes.
fact: «Her kindness has been of inestimable value to our family.»
fact: The phrase «of inestimable importance» means something matters more than we could ever measure.
exercise: «Your help has been of inestimable value.» What does inestimable mean here? | *Extremely valuable | Very cheap | Sometimes useful | Lost and never found
exercise: The word inestimable is formal. Where would you most likely meet it? | In a rhyme about a cat | In a text message about lunch | In a shopping list for milk and bread | *In a speech or a thank-you note
exercise: The parts in- and estimate suggest what about inestimable? | Something that is shouted loudly | Something that is wet and grey | *Something too great to be measured or counted | Something that is cheap and easy to buy
test: «The gift was of inestimable worth to the village.» What does this sentence tell you? | *The gift was too valuable to measure | The gift was very cheap | The gift was lost in the snow | The gift was given to nobody
test: Which phrase uses inestimable correctly? | Inestimable blue bicycle | Inestimable is my sandwich | Inestimable ran quickly home | *Of inestimable value
exam: Which sentence uses inestimable in the right sense? | Mum bought an inestimable pair of socks for a pound | *The old painting was of inestimable worth, so the museum guarded it carefully | I had an inestimable lunch of cheese and crisps | The inestimable rain fell all afternoon, so we stayed in
